+
+### 13.1 Answer to “is the delta acceptable?”
+
+**Five of the six drafting changes are satisfied. The sixth (R5) is correctly
+stated but not resolved.** Therefore I do **not** certify the delta as ready for
+publication/ballot: a valid enactment followed by an implementation commit
+still cannot lawfully land, because the pre-landing verification route does not
+exist. The bill is not the “only route out” until R5's prerequisite is supplied.
+
+This is not a request to weaken the bill. It is the opposite: the bill's own §4
+and §5 now require the pre-landing record, and that is the right standard. The
+Clerk should not publish the ballot request until one of these exists and is
+verified:
+
+- a reviewed pre-landing procedure for direct protected-record commits
+  (candidate tree verified before landing), or
+- a narrow enactment/records merger, or
+- an express new-law ordering rule.
+
+The current practice — direct single-parent commits to `LAWS.md` /
+`records/bills/*` — is not that route.
+
+### 13.2 Predictions
+
+The explicit P2 rule now matches §2.2.3(v): P2 is true only if the TSV is
+canonical and tally is run in the clean detached `64a466a` worktree; the live
+tree is excluded. That resolves the contradiction I identified earlier. P1
+still does not itself test pre-landing verification, but §4/§5 make that a
+binding condition; the omission is recorded in §7a. The five-prediction set is
+therefore acceptable as a calibration instrument, with the caveat already
+noted: it measures execution/process, not the legal validity of the route.
+
